<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  PROJECTOS-RECOVERY-INSTALLED-APP1A - the installer-owned installation record component.

  An ADDITIVE WiX fragment, referenced from tauri.conf.json through wix.fragmentPaths and
  wix.componentGroupRefs. Tauri's own generated main.wxs is untouched: there is no template
  override, no second installer, no custom action, no service and no privileged helper. One MSI.

  WHAT IT INSTALLS. Exactly one file, at the one canonical path the runtime reads:

      <ProgramData>\ProjectOS\host\installation\application.v1.json

  CommonAppDataFolder is the Windows Installer property for ProgramData, so the location comes from
  the platform rather than from an environment variable - which matches the runtime, where the same
  directory is resolved through SHGetKnownFolderPath and never through %ProgramData%.

  THE CONTENT IS NOT AUTHORED HERE. The record's JSON is emitted by `projectos-install-record`,
  which builds it from the canonical InstalledApplicationRecordV1 model, computes the digest with
  the same function the reader uses, and verifies the result with the real reader before writing.
  Hand-writing that JSON in XML would be a second copy of a versioned, digest-bound schema, kept in
  step by nothing but attention.

  UNINSTALL OWNS IT. The file is the component's KeyPath, so Windows Installer removes it when the
  product is uninstalled and rolls it back if installation fails. The three RemoveFolder entries
  take the directories with it, so no orphaned trust record and no empty ProjectOS tree survive an
  uninstall. Nothing here is permanent-by-default.

  WHAT IT GRANTS, STATED EXACTLY. The record carries installation facts only: no secret, no key
  material, no trust anchor, no signature algorithm and no project selection. A record without a
  verified application is worth nothing, because the governed root it names is only ever consumed
  through the sealed boundary that demands a VerifiedPackagedIdentity.

  It is not, however, true that this component grants nothing at all - it used to say so, and that
  stopped being accurate when the file below was given an explicit ACL. Normal users receive READ
  ONLY on this one file, because the installed Control Centre runs as a normal user and must read
  it. They receive no write, modify, delete or ownership rights, and the surrounding ProjectOSHost
  custody directories are unchanged. Read access to a corroborating fact is not a trust grant, and
  this comment should not be read as claiming more or less than that.

  This file is a TEMPLATE. `@RECORD_SOURCE@` is replaced with the absolute path of the generated
  record by packaging/build-release-payload.mjs. The generated .wxs is build output; this template
  is the reviewable source.
-->
<Wix xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/wix/2006/wi">
  <Fragment>
    <!-- ProgramData\ProjectOS\host\installation - the same segments the runtime's canonical path
         is built from, so there is one path vocabulary rather than two. -->
    <DirectoryRef Id="TARGETDIR">
      <Directory Id="CommonAppDataFolder">
        <Directory Id="PROJECTOS_PROGRAMDATA_DIR" Name="ProjectOS">
          <Directory Id="PROJECTOS_HOST_STATE_DIR" Name="host">
            <Directory Id="PROJECTOS_INSTALLATION_DIR" Name="installation" />
          </Directory>
        </Directory>
      </Directory>
    </DirectoryRef>

    <ComponentGroup Id="ProjectOSInstallationRecord">
      <Component Id="ProjectOSInstallationRecordFile"
                 Guid="{6F3C1B84-2E5A-4E31-9E2F-7C1A9D0B4F52}"
                 Directory="PROJECTOS_INSTALLATION_DIR"
                 Win64="yes">
        <File Id="ProjectOSApplicationRecordV1"
              Name="application.v1.json"
              Source="@RECORD_SOURCE@"
              KeyPath="yes">
          <!-- THE RECORD MUST BE READABLE BY THE APPLICATION THAT READS IT.
               ================================================================================
               Authored because relying on inheritance FAILED IN PRACTICE. The first real
               installation put this file under a ProgramData tree the ProjectOSHost custody work
               had already hardened: owner NT SERVICE\ProjectOSHost, inheritance PROTECTED,
               non-inheriting ACEs. This component authored no ACL, so the file inherited NOTHING
               and ended up readable only by SYSTEM and msiserver - unreadable even to an elevated
               administrator, and therefore unreadable to the Control Centre. Installation
               evidence became Unavailable and packaged identity could not complete. Fail-closed,
               correctly, but the product did not work.

               THE FIX IS THIS ONE FILE, NOT THE TREE. The surrounding ProjectOSHost custody
               directories are deliberately NOT touched: no directory-wide relaxation, no change
               of ownership, no weakening of the host's own identity.v1.json. A file-specific
               descriptor is the narrowest thing that solves the problem.

                 D:P                 protected - inherits nothing, so a future change to a parent
                                     cannot silently widen or narrow this file
                 (A;;FA;;;SY)        LocalSystem       full control
                 (A;;FA;;;BA)        Administrators    full control
                 (A;;FR;;;BU)        Users             GENERIC READ ONLY

               USERS GET READ, AND NOTHING ELSE. No write, append, modify, delete, WRITE_DAC or
               WRITE_OWNER; no Everyone and no Authenticated Users entry at all. A normal user can
               read this record and cannot alter it, which matters because a user-writable record
               would be a self-signed claim about the installation rather than evidence about it.

               WHAT READ ACCESS DOES NOT GRANT. The record still carries no secret, no key
               material, no trust anchor, no signature algorithm and no project selection - only
               installation facts. It CORROBORATES; it cannot mint trust. The governed root it
               names is reachable only through the sealed boundary that demands a
               VerifiedPackagedIdentity, which comes from the owner-signed manifest verified
               against the anchor compiled into the application. Being able to read this file buys
               a reader exactly nothing it did not already have.

               Core WiX PermissionEx, which emits the standard MsiLockPermissionsEx table. NOT
               util:PermissionEx - that is the custom-action-based SecureObjects element, and this
               installer authors no custom action. Requires Windows Installer 5.0, which is why
               the package declares InstallerVersion 500. -->
          <PermissionEx Sddl="D:P(A;;FA;;;SY)(A;;FA;;;BA)(A;;FR;;;BU)" />
        </File>

        <!-- Take the directories back on uninstall, innermost first. Without these the empty
             ProjectOS tree would remain in ProgramData after the product is gone. -->
        <RemoveFolder Id="RemoveProjectOSInstallationDir"
                      Directory="PROJECTOS_INSTALLATION_DIR"
                      On="uninstall" />
        <RemoveFolder Id="RemoveProjectOSHostStateDir"
                      Directory="PROJECTOS_HOST_STATE_DIR"
                      On="uninstall" />
        <RemoveFolder Id="RemoveProjectOSProgramDataDir"
                      Directory="PROJECTOS_PROGRAMDATA_DIR"
                      On="uninstall" />
      </Component>
    </ComponentGroup>
  </Fragment>
</Wix>
